How to create a login form in JavaScript

A login form has three layers working together: HTML structure, CSS styling, and JavaScript behavior. The live preview shows the finished result.
1. Build the HTML structure

Use semantic form elements so browsers and screen readers understand it:

xml
<form id="loginForm" novalidate>
  <div class="field">
    <label for="email">Email address</label>
    <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required />
  </div>
  <div class="field">
    <label for="password">Password</label>
    <input type="password" id="password" name="password" minlength="6" required />
    <button type="button" id="togglePass">Show</button>
  </div>
  <button type="submit">Sign in</button>
</form>

Key points: type="email" and type="password" give you native browser behavior for free. Every <input> has a matching <label>. The novalidate attribute turns off the browser's default bubbles so you can write your own validation.
2. Validate on submit

The core of a JS login form is the submit event handler. You check the inputs, show errors inline, and only "log in" if everything passes:

javascript
const form = document.getElementById('loginForm');
const email = document.getElementById('email');
const password = document.getElementById('password');
const emailRe = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;

form.addEventListener('submit', function (e) {
  e.preventDefault();              // stop the page reloading

  const emailOk = emailRe.test(email.value.trim());
  const passOk = password.value.length >= 6;

  // show / hide error messages
  emailField.classList.toggle('invalid', !emailOk);
  passwordField.classList.toggle('invalid', !passOk);

  if (!emailOk || !passOk) return;  // stop if invalid

  // valid — simulate the login request
  submitBtn.disabled = true;
  setTimeout(() => {
    // show success state
  }, 1200);
});

e.preventDefault() is the most important line — without it the form reloads the page and your JavaScript never runs.
3. Add a password show/hide toggle

Toggle the input's type attribute between password and text:

javascript
togglePass.addEventListener('click', function () {
  const show = password.type === 'password';
  password.type = show ? 'text' : 'password';
  togglePass.setAttribute('aria-label', show ? 'Hide password' : 'Show password');
});

4. Handle the loading and success states

Real logins take a moment, so disable the button and show a spinner while you "wait":

javascript
submitBtn.classList.add('loading');
submitBtn.disabled = true;
setTimeout(() => {
  form.classList.add('hidden');
  document.getElementById('success').classList.add('show');
}, 1200);

5. Clear errors as the user types

Don't leave red errors visible after the user starts fixing them:

javascript
email.addEventListener('input', () => {
  if (emailField.classList.contains('invalid')) validateEmail();
});

Important note

This validates on the client only — it's a UI demo. A real login system needs a backend/API that checks credentials against a database, hashes passwords, and issues session tokens. Never store or verify passwords in the browser.

